    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d279">

                <pb facs="0360" n="262" xml:id="pg262"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.01 Manchuria/474: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Chargé in Great Britain (Atherton)</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-09-17T15:00:00-04:00">September 17, 1932—3
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>246. Your 271, September 16, 1 p.m., first sentence. For your information and
                    guidance.</p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>Japanese Government apparently has made no formal notification. Tokyo
                        informs us that information in regard to recognition was given out there in
                        the form of a public statement. Apparently no official notification to
                        Embassy. Here, the Japanese Embassy has simply handed an officer of the
                        Department a copy, without covering note or memorandum and without comment,
                        of what appears to be a mimeographed press release.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>The Department is taking no official cognizance and making no comment, for
                        the present at least.</item>
                </list>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Stimson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
